{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.prompt.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 23:00 +07\n# Task: slice 7c, the composition and the compose and fencing rows (codex implements)\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7c`, branch `srvm-phase7c`, from the integration branch `srvm-phase7` (= master + 7a custody on rows + 7b holder export/recovery v2/driver lower/install checks). 7a-7d land on `srvm-phase7`; master only when 7d is green, so suites 7c/7d own are red until you re-base them. Your work will be reviewed by a NON-codex reviewer (Fable) with mutants: make every mutant below catchable by a test. Read first: `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`; `collab/phase7-plan.md` (3.4 crash cuts, 3.5 publication of successors, 3.6 authority vs sealed refs, 3.8, 3.9, section 4 slice 7c is your scope); `collab/7a-report.md`; `collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md` (what exists now: export prepare/encode, recovery v2, rehydrate-fenced, the driver, `source-index` as a required composition seam); `collab/7b-review.md` (Fable's findings F1-F3: F1 and F2 are test additions YOU land first as the slice's first commit-sized step, F3 is your re-base); `docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7, 7.11; `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` (as amended); `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/{compose,rows,holder/*,authority/*,custody,ledger}.cljc`. Owner rulings: fork custody nil; the REPL custody gap is accepted until phase 8 (you do NOT touch `yin.repl/main.cljc`: the plain-composition test is re-based on a semantic-register receiver and the REPL's `:custody` branch is only documented inert, report it); code and trees load through the linker's source-index/fetch/admission (no resolver of your own); A is the only code identity; BLAKE3; no compat shims or version dispatch; lease/epoch/holder never enter a body; the de Bruijn kernels are backends with a documented fragment and are not widened (do not touch them).\nDo: (0) F1: in `holder/driver_test`'s fence test assert the two `:yin.k/store` intents appear in journal order with `(:yin.k/record fence)` first and `(:yin.k/address fence)` second; F2: add the Q3 record-address case (`(assoc-in [:response :derivation :yin.ledger/profile :x] 1)` expecting `:install-image`) to `rows_custody_test`; (1) re-base `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; F3: replace `:export-version 99` in the smuggled-key test with `:source-index (fn [] {})`; the plain-composition test per the owner ruling), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack; the pack address is the store key and the root id inside the baseline), `handoff_v2_authority_test` (the 7a defect D1: it breaks under any option: re-base it on rows or delete it, state which and why: it tests the old inspector/codec which stays until phase 8, so prefer deleting only what tests the version-1/2 AUTHORITY path that no longer exists and keep whatever still tests the old codec directly); (2) the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` re-based and green; (3) if production code needs a change to make these suites meaningful (compose.cljc seams, authority paths), make the smallest change and report it. If the plan and the code disagree, the code wins: report as a design defect, never silently change semantics. Portability: `.cljc` for JVM, Node, Dart (no `for` over possibly long seqs, no private var access, `(assoc {} ...)`, no unary minus on floats, `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` order, catches `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)`); slow cases `^:slow` + `dao.test-slow/guard`.\nVerification, ALL foreground, JVM only, never background a lane: `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`; `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <each touched ns>` and `-i :slow` (DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 where the suite uses it); every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace: report which still fail (only 7d's Stage E suites may remain red); `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test` unchanged (no root id moves). No Node/Dart. Mutants (cp backup; diff and `git diff --quiet` to verify restore): recovery stored after the pack; a custody refusal after attach; a grant check skipped in the compose path; fencing lower without the exporting gate; offer carrying the wrong pack address; a content-store cut meaning swapped; source-index ignored in compose; report accepting an unfenced baseline. Which mutants do the tests catch? Do not commit. Report `collab/1792480000000-engineer-7c-compose-fencing.report.md`: files with line counts, test counts, mutants, deviations, design defects (separate list), what 7d needs.\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md collab/7b-review.md collab/7a-report.md collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md collab/phase7-plan.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md collab/7b-review.md collab/7a-report.md collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md collab/phase7-plan.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 22:10 +07\n# Review findings: slice 7b, the holder's export, recovery v2, the driver's lower, install checks\n\nReviewer: claude-fable-5-1 (non-author; codex wrote 7b, opus the retained partial edit). Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b`, branch `srvm-phase7b`, commit `95c228c0` over `srvm-phase7`. Method: full read of `git diff srvm-phase7..HEAD` (15 files), the plan (§3.2-3.5, 3.8, slice 7b), the 7a report, codex's 7b report and the brief; the quick and slow lanes of the named suites; a probe of the deferred suites; 18 mutants over `export.cljc`, `rows.cljc` and `driver.cljc` with byte-level restore verification. JVM only. Nothing in the repo was edited; review artifacts live under `target/7b-review/` (ignored).\n\n## Verification I ran\n\n| Lane | Command | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Quick | `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n` export, driver, writer, reader, evidence, rows-custody, rows-checkpoint, rows, safepoint-harness, layout, lift-v1, handoff-v2-holder | 329 tests, 7250 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| Slow | `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n` the ten suites of the brief | 5 tests, 3113 assertions, 0 failures |\n| Lint | `clj -M:kondo --lint` the 12 changed `.cljc` files | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| Probe | the deferred suites plus checkpoint, ledger-fixture, crash-cut, grant, completion, admission, store-write-audit | see below |\n\nSuites that stay red until 7c/7d (per `Testing` section of the probe log): `compose-rows-test` 39, `compose-test` 23, `fencing-rows-test` 31, `stage-e-rows-test` 5 (quick ones), `handoff-v2-authority-test` 8 (7a D1, needs an owner disposition). Every other probed suite is green: `checkpoint-test`, `ledger-fixture-test`, `crash-cut-test`, `authority.{grant,completion,admission}-test`, `store-write-audit-test`. This matches codex's table exactly; nothing else regressed.\n\n## What I checked, item by item\n\n1. **`holder/export.cljc`.** `prepare`/`encode` lift through `rows/lift-task` with `:header`, `:source-index` and `:serve-keyed` (`rows.cljc` threads the task path through `install-node` so child answers are keyed `[['host.mod] sid]`). `encode`'s server throws on an unprepared key and never calls `serve!`. D4 is honoured: the lift serves during the walk, the `once` closure records every answer before the lift's post-walk refusal, and a retry is seeded by handle from the retained table through the machine's bindings (`seeded-by-handle`), so no successful serve repeats (mutant M13 killed by four tests). Recovery v2 carries exactly `{export-recovery, version, pack-address, root, pack-bytes, header, state}`; `state` is `{gate origins issues issued children}` recursively. No snapshot body, no descriptor set, no lease/epoch/holder (the header's `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's lease as lineage evidence, which the plan allows; the pack never carries `:yin.k/enrolled`, pinned by test). The deviation from the plan's sketch (no `:yin.k/descriptors`) follows the owner's \"only holder-local engine state beside the pack\" and is sound: rehydration rebuilds the served table from the attached descriptors.\n2. **Rehydration.** Order in `rehydrate-fenced`: tag/version, recovery address, `checkpoint/inspect` (pack address vs bytes, carrier, custody grammar), root equality, `check-header!`, `header-agrees?` against the inspected custody, `valid-state?` against the decoded tree, then `lower-task {:fenced true}`. All before the first `attach` (tests pin `before = @attaches`). The fenced lower carries `id-counter` exactly (M03 killed, 68 assertions), builds `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census, moves waits and parked records out of the scheduler (M04 killed), gates every task `:exporting`; `extract-recovered` restamps issues and strips custody/closes/counters from the receiver. Zero program IO: M02d (ungated poll of the recovered waits) killed by six tests; M02b (rows-level fenced gate dropped) killed by `rows-custody-test` only, because `extract-recovered` re-asserts the gate itself (defence in depth, not a gap). `:make-stream` is nilled on a copy of the receiver passed to `spawn-module` and restored on the child; nothing is mutated, so there is no leak on exception. The recovered record re-encodes the published pack byte for byte (every rehydration test asserts `reproduces?`); a reordered-row pack of the same root is refused `:recovery-inconsistent`. Abort of a recovered record is refused `:restarted` (M05 killed).\n3. **Q3 install checks.** `check-install!` requires `parent = (:yin.link/id response)` and a `:derivation` record whose address is the manifest's `:yin.semantic/code` entry, whose input is the manifest's tree and whose output is `segment-key` of the delivered image. The response shape (top-level `:manifest`, `:derivation`, `:image {:value}`) is exactly what `yin.vm.engine` reads at install (engine.cljc 1209, 1295, 1394) and what `linker/link-manifest` emits (`linked-image` merges `:manifest`, `:derivation`, `:trust` beside fetch's `:image`), so a valid linker-produced install is not refused; `:trusted` links also carry the record. A forged install passes only if manifest, record and image are mutually consistent: the check is self-consistency within the response, which is what the owner asked for (\"image address = the manifest's\"); binding the manifest to a composition-known address is the linker's admission, not the lower's. M06 and M07 (each check removed whole) killed; the three conjuncts of the image check separately: tree input (M07b) and image output (M07c) killed, record address (M07a, M07a2) **survived**, see F2.\n4. **The driver.** `lowered` wraps `rows/lower-task` with the refusal-as-data contract; `lower` passes `:exclusive`, `:protection`, `:grant {:checkpoint address ...}`; `step-validating` inspects first, then lowers under `:exclusive` with no grant (awaiting-grant before any attachment; halted → `:safepoint` ended); `rebuilt-cell` checks `:yin.k/pack-address`, header equality, bytes vs base64 pack, then `rehydrate-fenced` and re-encode equality; `reopen-holder`/`reopen-exit` read kind/occurrence through the rows inspector. `:export-version` is gone from `driver.cljc` and `compose.cljc` (seam list, `bare-composition`, `driver-config`, docstrings); `source-index` is a required composition seam (`check!`) and a required driver seam. Occurrence minting and journaling precede prepare (unchanged `source-mint`/`exit-mint`); `export-fence-step` stores the recovery object first, then the pack (M11 **survived** at the test level, see F1); journal vocabulary unchanged, and renaming a journal tag or the store action (M12a/M12b) is refused by the driver's closed frame vocabulary so loudly that the test process cannot even load the suite (killed at process level).\n5. **Deletions.** `lift_v1_test`: the five deleted deftests (`a-malformed-header…`, `two-encodes…`, `the-served-table…`, `a-retry-serves-no-already-served-handle…`, `the-record-round-trips…`) reappear verbatim in `export_test` over `rows_support` machines (`parked-writer-shared`/`-late` ported); the child-hold case now calls `export/enter` directly; `throws?` helper removed with them. `lift_support/prepared` removed and `lift` now calls `handoff/export-task` directly, so the old codec is exercised with no holder wrapper (codex D3, correct under \"no shims\"). `handoff_v2_holder_test`: only `a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced` deleted, as the plan lists. `driver_test`: `a-version-two-fork-lift…` deleted as planned; `profile-refusal-precedes-wrong-address-test` becomes the two-address variant pinning profile-mismatch vs hash-mismatch with zero attaches. The old codec and inspector are untouched.\n6. **`rows.cljc` and fixtures.** The 66 added lines are: `:serve-keyed`/`:path` plumbing (lift), the Q3 checks (stronger, not weaker), `with-custody`'s fenced parked move, `spawn-child`'s fenced constructor, the exact fenced `id-counter`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, and docstrings. `rows_support`: `linked-response` (manifest tree + derivation record, mirroring `link-manifest`), two shared-handle builders. The harness change is `module-response` data only. `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`: exactly three pins changed (`installs`, `child-header`, `child-op-id-out-of-range`), all install-bearing, as the extended install response requires; no other root moved (harness and `rows_test` green). `rows_custody_test` gains the Q3 deftest.\n7. **Red suites**: listed above.\n8. **Portability read.** No `for`, no private-var access across namespaces, catches `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` everywhere new, `#?(:cljd … :clj …)` order kept, `(assoc {} …)` starts, no unary float minus, no EDN strings. Base64 through `jing/bytes->base64`/`base64->bytes` (the shared `dao.stream` codec, all hosts); byte equality by `(vec …)`; `integer?` on CBOR-decoded counters is host-safe for whole numbers. `yin.vm.engine/…` is never referenced from `export.cljc` (the surviving M02c mutant used it; the production file does not). `(:yin.link/id response)` and `parent` round-trip CBOR as vectors of keyword and int on every host. Unverified on Node/Dart by instruction.\n\n## Mutants (mine)\n\nDriver: `target/7b-review/mut.py`; each mutant's diff and log under `target/7b-review/mutants/`; restore verified by byte copy from a pristine backup plus `git diff --quiet -- src`; final `git status` shows no tracked change.\n\n| Mutant | Killed? | By |\n|---|---|---|\n| M01 freeze keeps a snapshot body | yes (1) | `the-recovery-object-is-the-pack-and-holder-local-state-test` |\n| M02 rehydrate `vm/run`s the recovered machine (gated) | inert (0) | `vm/run` on a gated blocked machine does nothing; not a real mutant |\n| M02b rows fenced gate and wait move dropped | yes (2) | `an-exclusive-blocked-root-under-fenced-skips-the-grant` only; export tests masked by `extract-recovered` |\n| M02c rehydrate polls waits under gate `:running` | inert (0) | a gated machine observes nothing in `check-wait-set`; not a real mutant |\n| M02d rehydrate polls the waits ungated (program IO) | yes (6) | the four zero-call rehydration tests, `complete-export-recovery…`, the FFI route test |\n| M03 fenced id counter off by one | yes (68) | `the-id-counter-is-carried-exactly-test`, every `reproduces?` |\n| M04 a parked record dropped at fenced lower | yes (14) | `recovery-retains-every-parked-record-test`, three more |\n| M05 restarted abort accepted | yes (1) | `a-restarted-abort-is-refused-test` |\n| M06 Q3 link-id check removed | yes (4) | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| M07 Q3 image check removed whole | yes (10) | same |\n| M07a / M07a2 Q3 record-address conjunct removed or weakened to `contains?` | **no** (0) | none: every \"another record\" case also changes the tree or the output |\n| M07b Q3 tree-input conjunct removed | yes (2) | same deftest |\n| M07c Q3 image-output conjunct removed | yes (4) | same deftest |\n| M08 source index ignored (nil to the lift) | yes (91) | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test`, every lift |\n| M09 attach before custody in `lower-task` | yes (30) | the four zero-attach deftests of `rows_custody_test` |\n| M10 `:make-stream` not restored on a fenced child | **no** (0) | equivalent mutant: a fenced recovery never executes, so the child's constructor is never read |\n| M11 recovery object stored after the pack | **no** (0) | no 7b suite observes the two-put order; only `compose_rows_test` (red until 7c) does |\n| M12a journal tag `:yin.k/fenced` renamed | yes (process) | the driver's closed frame vocabulary throws at load |\n| M12b store action `:yin.k/store` renamed | yes (process) | same |\n| M13 serve repeated on retry (retained table and handle seed dropped) | yes (7) | `a-retry-serves-no-already-served-handle-test`, `a-refused-prepare-keeps-what-it-served-test`, two more |\n\nCodex's nine mutants are consistent with mine where they overlap (M01, M02d, M03, M04, M05, M06, M07, M08, M09).\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | severity | file:line | finding | fix |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | medium (test gap) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc:911` | The order \"recovery object first, then the pack\" is implemented but no green suite protects it: swapping the two `store-object` calls passes all 116 driver deftests (M11). Until 7c re-bases `compose_rows_test`, the crash-cut meaning of `store-put-*` vs `body-put-*` rests on reading the code. | In `driver_test`'s fence test (around line 3184) assert the two `:yin.k/store` intents appear in journal order with `(:yin.k/record fence)` first and `(:yin.k/address fence)` second. Five lines; can land with 7c. |\n| F2 | low (test gap) | `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:289` | The Q3 record-address conjunct (`segment-key record` = the manifest's `:yin.semantic/code` entry) is not isolated: replacing it with `(contains? manifest :yin.module/derivations)` passes (M07a2), because \"a derivation record the manifest does not name\" swaps in a record whose tree and output also differ, so the other conjuncts refuse first. | Add a case whose record keeps the honest input and output but differs in content (e.g. `(assoc-in [:response :derivation :yin.ledger/profile :x] 1)`), expecting `:install-image`. |\n| F3 | low (7c note) | `test/yin/vm/ucf/compose_test.cljc:614` | The smuggled-key test still lists `:export-version 99` expecting the \"is a composition seam\" refusal; with the key removed from `holder-config-keys` this assertion will fail once 7c re-bases the suite. Not a 7b regression (the suite is red for other reasons) but it must not be mistaken for one. | 7c: replace the entry with `:source-index (fn [] {})`. |\n| F4 | info | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:2074` | Restoring `:make-stream` on a fenced child is unobservable (M10): a fenced recovery never executes the child, and the relift does not read the constructor. Harmless hygiene; keep or drop. | None required. |\n| F5 | info (design note) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:1845` | The Q3 image check is self-consistency of manifest, record and image within the response; it does not bind the manifest to an address the composition knows, so a response that forges all three together passes. This is the owner's stated scope (link-id = parent, image = the manifest's) and admission stays the linker's; recorded so 7e documents the boundary. | 7e: say so in continuation §11 Q3's closing text. |\n| F6 | info | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc:470` | `extract-recovered` re-asserts gate `:exporting` and an empty wait set, so `export_test` cannot see whether `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}` gated the tree (M02b was caught only by `rows_custody_test`). Defence in depth, acceptable; noted so nobody deletes the rows-level test as redundant. | None. |\n\nNo correctness defect found in the production code. The owner's rulings hold: A is the only code identity (trees from the composition's index, no resolver built), the AST is never lost (M08 refuses without the index), BLAKE3 for pack and recovery addresses, no version dispatch, no compat shim, lease/epoch/holder never in a body, fork custody nil, `yin.repl` untouched (it names `compose` only in docstrings).\n\nVERDICT: READY_TO_MERGE (into the integration branch `srvm-phase7`). F1 and F2 are test additions, non-blocking for integration; land them with 7c or as a small follow-up before 7c's review. F3 is 7c's.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n\nWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n\n## Verdict\n\n7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n\n**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n\n  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n\n  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n\n## Files\n\nCreated:\n\n| File | Lines | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n\nChanged:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n- Fixture bodies only:\n  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n\n## What the code does (for review)\n\n- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n  - nil on every child.\n- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n\n  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n- **Restore:**\n  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n- **The two identities:**\n  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n\n## Mutation checks\n\nDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n\n## Design defects found (separate list)\n\n- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n  - Options for the orchestrator:\n    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n\n## What 7b needs from me\n\n- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7b: holder export, recovery v2, and driver lower\n\nImplemented and verified in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b`, branch `srvm-phase7b`. No commit. JVM only; every test process ran in the foreground, sequentially, including the mutation runs. No Node, Dart, remote host, network fetch, or escalation was needed.\n\n## Result\n\n7b's acceptance passes. The holder exports row packs under the composition's source index, freezes recovery format 2 with one pack and holder-local metadata, and rehydrates through fenced row lowering without program IO or child initialization. Exact counters, cursor aliases/distinct cells, all parked records, authentic receiver-owned values, operation IDs and issue stamps survive recovery. The recovered record reproduces the published pack byte for byte and cannot abort into execution. Q3's link/parent and manifest/image checks refuse before attachment. The driver's validation, grant lower and restart paths use rows; `:export-version` is removed from the driver/composition.\n\nThe existing partial edit was reviewed first with `git diff`, then by production-file review and tests. Its row export/driver/recovery implementation was retained. I removed its copied legacy holder export implementation from `lift_support`, moved the five preparation tests to the row holder suite, and added a two-record parked recovery test. The legacy codec is exercised directly through its existing `handoff/export-task`; no compatibility shim or old recovery reader was added.\n\nThis is integration-branch readiness for **7b**, not readiness to land the entire phase on master. The deferred red suites are listed below.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nCounts are final file lines and added/deleted lines versus this worktree's HEAD, including the retained partial edit.\n\n| File | Final lines | Added | Deleted |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc` | 1009 | 11 | 6 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc` | 2943 | 82 | 56 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc` | 628 | 302 | 242 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | 2262 | 66 | 23 |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 946 | 306 | 277 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | 1939 | 11 | 2 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` | 217 | 5 | 156 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4255 | 45 | 45 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export_test.cljc` | 974 | 625 | 563 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader_test.cljc` | 1445 | 1 | 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` | 1127 | 6 | 5 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_support.cljc` | 454 | 3 | 21 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_v1_test.cljc` | 471 | 2 | 138 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 315 | 40 | 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 297 | 64 | 5 |\n\n`holder/evidence_test.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, and `layout_test.cljc` needed no edit. The harness change only supplies complete linker-style manifest/derivation data for Q3's install fixtures; its existing assertions and root pins were not edited. The rows checkpoint resource was regenerated for the extended install responses and is verified against its renderer. Production `handoff.cljc` and the old inspector/codec implementations remain untouched.\n\n## Implementation details that matter to review\n\n- `holder.export/prepare` retains served answers by `[task-path resource-id]`, resolves handle aliases from the retained record, and passes `:header`, `:source-index` and `:serve-keyed` to `rows/lift-task`. `encode` reads only those answers; it never serves externally.\n- Recovery format 2 contains exactly the tag, version, pack address, structural root, base64 pack, local header, and `:yin.k/state` (gate, origins, issued, ordered issues, child metadata). There is no second snapshot body, duplicate descriptor set, current lease, epoch or holder in the pack. The origin's predecessor lease remains as the specified lineage evidence.\n- Rehydration validates the recovery address, pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape before attachment. Fenced lower preserves the exact carried id counter, records resource-key/cursor-cell associations, detaches waits and every parked record, and gates every task exporting. Child construction disables `:make-stream` during administrative spawning, restoring the composition's constructor afterward.\n- Q3 checks the response's `:yin.link/id` against the install parent and checks its image through the manifest-named semantic derivation record: record address, tree input and image output. It adds no fetcher, resolver or initialization path.\n- The driver still mints and journals occurrences before prepare, stores recovery before the pack, and preserves the existing fence/offer/report/release brackets and crash-cut names. The source index is a required composition seam, supplied as `(fn [] {A #{tree-address}})`.\n- 7a D4 is preserved: lift serves during its walk before deciding exhaustion/protection refusals. The record retains those answers, and the retry test proves no successful serve is repeated.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll changed Clojure files were linted with `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`: **0 errors, 0 warnings**. `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nUnique successful namespace coverage (excluding duplicate focused/post-mutation runs): **514 quick tests / 8,941 assertions**, plus **5 slow tests / 3,113 assertions**, all with zero failures and errors. Each touched namespace was run using `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns>` and `-i :slow`; the two support namespaces correctly select zero tests. All five holder namespaces and all eight authority namespaces were included. The rows checkpoint suite was also run in both tiers.\n\n| Namespace | Quick tests / assertions | Slow tests / assertions |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test` | 12 / 2396 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test` | 23 / 1213 | 3 / 2716 |\n| `yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 3 / 129 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission-test` | 23 / 240 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-fold-test` | 8 / 50 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-test` | 25 / 303 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.front-test` | 30 / 217 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant-test` | 16 / 107 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.inherited-test` | 18 / 112 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.input-test` | 20 / 125 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.reclaim-test` | 21 / 108 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint-test` | 15 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-holder-test` | 5 / 338 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` | 116 / 1524 | 1 / 389 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence-test` | 9 / 38 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.export-test` | 36 / 325 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader-test` | 24 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer-test` | 25 / 202 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixture-test` | 6 / 53 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-v1-test` | 21 / 208 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test` | 17 / 179 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test` | 12 / 127 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 29 / 453 | 1 / 8 |\n\nThe driver has **117 deftests total** (116 quick, one slow), as required. Rows, layout, and the safepoint harness retain their assertions; no existing pinned root was changed. The store-write audit, old checkpoint suite, old direct v1 lift tests and remaining v2 holder tests also pass. After restoring every mutant, the focused export/custody/checkpoint gate passed again: **65 tests / 631 assertions**.\n\nLogs and machine-readable results: `target/7b-verification/`, `results.json`, `final-checks.json`, `final-lint.log`, and `mutants/results.json`. These are local ignored verification artifacts.\n\n### Deferred red suites, observed unchanged\n\nThese quick-tier probes were run to identify the integration boundary; they are excluded from the green acceptance totals. No fixes were made in these suites.\n\n| Namespace | Tests / assertions | Failures / errors | Follow-up |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-test` | 14 / 23 | 3 / 20 | 7c: row machines and source-index wiring |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-rows-test` | 39 / 39 | 0 / 39 | 7c: row machines, source-index and recovery-v2 assertions |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.fencing-rows-test` | 4 / 61 | 29 / 2 | 7c: row lift/lower and rows authority inspector |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 5 / 5 | 0 / 5 | 7d: port the world and its composition seams |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-authority-test` | 3 / 68 | 8 / 0 | Known 7a D1: old v2 bodies are refused by the rows-only authority |\n\nThe Stage E process suite was not run: it requires the JVM/Node peer matrix, prohibited by this brief's JVM-only/no-Node instruction. Deferred compose/Stage E slow tiers belong to 7c/7d and were not run here. `yin.repl` was not touched, per owner Q2.\n\n## Mutations\n\nEach mutant ran in a fresh foreground JVM against `holder.export-test` or `rows-custody-test`. Sources were copied to backups first; each mutant's diff and test log were saved. After each run, restoration was checked byte for byte, with `git diff --quiet --no-index <backup> <source>`, and by comparing the entire binary worktree diff with its pre-mutation diff. Plain `git diff --quiet` against HEAD cannot be clean in this intentionally uncommitted worktree; no-index verifies restoration without erasing the intended edit. All nine behavior-changing mutants were killed; none survived.\n\n| Mutant | Failures / errors | Representative catching test |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `snapshot-body` | 1 / 0 | `the-recovery-object-is-the-pack-and-holder-local-state-test` |\n| `program-execution` | 6 / 0 | `complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible-test` |\n| `counter-off-by-one` | 64 / 4 | `the-id-counter-is-carried-exactly-test` |\n| `parked-record-dropped` | 14 / 0 | `recovery-retains-every-parked-record-test` |\n| `restart-abort-accepted` | 1 / 0 | `a-restarted-abort-is-refused-test` |\n| `install-link-check-removed` | 4 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `install-image-check-removed` | 10 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `source-index-omitted` | 81 / 10 | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test` |\n| `attach-before-custody` | 30 / 0 | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` |\n\nThe program-execution mutant reinstated recovery waits, removed the exporting gate and called `vm/run` during rehydration; the zero-IO assertions caught the resulting operations. The attachment-order mutant attached first and then ran custody; 30 zero-attachment assertions failed. Snapshot duplication was detected by the recovery object's exact-key assertion. The Q3 checks were removed separately, not together.\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Five old-suite preparation tests moved to `holder.export-test`.** They exercised the retired holder wrapper, not merely the old codec. The initial partial edit copied that wrapper into test support. I removed the copy and moved the tests onto real semantic-register machines; direct v0/v1 coverage remains on the old codec. `lift_support/lift` now calls the existing codec directly, and the child hold/path assertion calls the kernel-agnostic `export/enter`.\n2. **Q3 required fixture completion beyond holder files.** `rows_support` and the harness's module-response builder now carry the manifest tree, derivation address and record. The row checkpoint resource therefore changes for install-containing cases. Existing row/layout root pins remain unchanged.\n3. **No duplicate recovery descriptor set.** The pack already carries every stream descriptor. Rehydration derives the internal retained served table from those descriptors and administrative attachments. This follows the owner's “only holder-local engine state beside the pack” instruction and avoids persisting the same facts twice.\n4. **Header validation belongs to `rehydrate-fenced`.** It compares the local recovery header with the inspected pack custody and validates `:yin.k/state` against the task tree before calling `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`. Raw row lowering does not take a recovery envelope or implement an independent recovery-header reader.\n5. **An additional all-parked-record test** covers an inactive record alongside the selected explicit park; both must survive with no IO and an identical re-encoded pack.\n\n## Design defects / discrepancies (separate list)\n\n- **D1, inherited from 7a:** the rows-only authority rejects old bodies. Compose/fencing/Stage E still need their planned ports, and the old v2 authority test is not repaired by those ports. The latter needs an explicit owner disposition before final phase acceptance; no version dispatch was introduced.\n- **D2, inherited from 7a:** the plan's different-root/equal-baseline variant conflicts with the actual baseline, which includes the root. Driver equal variants use a different row order with the same root; different roots remain conflicts. Recovery must reproduce canonical emitted bytes, so a merely reordered pack is valid carrier syntax but inconsistent recovery. The existing semantics were preserved and tested.\n- **D3, scope discrepancy:** the plan describes `lift_v1_test` as direct old-codec coverage to keep untouched, but five tests invoked the replaced holder preparation path. Keeping them unchanged would require the forbidden legacy wrapper/shim. Moving those five laws onto rows preserves their subjects without keeping the retired implementation alive.\n- **D4, recovery shape discrepancy:** plan §3.2's sketch includes a separate descriptor set; the owner brief says the pack plus only holder-local state. The implemented format omits the redundant set. Document this final shape in 7e.\n- **D5, known 7a lift ordering fact:** streams may already be served when a custody/protection lift refusal is returned. Accounting and retries preserve that fact; no zero-serve law was invented.\n\nNo additional unresolved production defect was found in the reviewed 7b implementation. Cross-host execution remains unverified here by instruction, not implied by the JVM results.\n\n## What 7c needs\n\n- Supply `:source-index` to `compose/open!` and driver configs as a function returning the composition/linker's current `{A #{tree-address}}`. Do not add a fallback resolver or synthetic index in production.\n- Build source and receiver machines on semantic-register, and use rows for fork/exclusive bodies. The holder answers pack bytes and their BLAKE3 **pack address**; the structural root is distinct and stays in the baseline.\n- Rebase content-store/restart assertions on recovery format 2: `:yin.k/pack-bytes`, `:yin.k/pack-address`, `:yin.k/root`, header and local state; no snapshot or duplicate descriptor set. Keep recovery-first/pack-second puts and the existing journal cut names.\n- Pass grant/protection/exclusive options to row lower. Preserve the no-attachment-before-custody law. Q3 install fixtures must carry a parent-correlated link ID and the manifest-named semantic derivation/image chain.\n- Use same-root/reordered-row packs for equal variants; use different-root packs only as conflicts. Preserve the accepted REPL gap until phase 8.\n- Run the deferred compose/fencing suites and their slow tiers, then hand the world/source-index/recovery changes to 7d for the Stage E matrix. No files in those suites or in `yin.repl` were edited here.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 16:10 +07\n# Phase 7 plan: Stage D/E rerun over the row carrier (Architect, plan only)\n\nAuthor: claude-fable-5-1. Worktree `srvm-phase7-plan` at `781a9ba8` (phases 1-6 merged). Read: the four design documents named in the brief, `rows.cljc`, `handoff.cljc` (export-task, the reader, resume-task*, the recovery paths), `holder/{export,driver,writer,reader}.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`, the Stage E world, scenarios, peer, process test and rows test, the checkpoint and ledger fixtures and tests, `lift_support.cljc`, `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc`, the semantic-register safepoint harness, `dao.jing.{file,mem}` put contracts, `yin.repl`'s composition wiring. No code or doc was edited.\n\n## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n\n1. Every Stage D/E test today runs on the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel through `handoff.cljc` bodies (version 0 stream-CBOR, version 1 and 2 jing-CBOR); the row carrier (version 3) runs only on the semantic-register kernel and only with `custody nil`.\n2. Phase 7 is therefore one move, done in four slices: teach the row carrier custody, re-base the holder (export record, recovery object, driver lower) and the authority's inspector on row packs, then rerun the composition and the Stage E crash matrix on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts.\n3. The driver, writer, reader, compose, authority, ledger and front are **kernel-agnostic**: they read machine fields that `yin.vm.engine` owns (`:wait-set`, `:installs`, `:yin.k/gate`, `:yin.k/custody`). They change only where they call `handoff/*` or `checkpoint/inspect` (eight call sites).\n4. Nothing of version 0/1/2 is rewritten; it dies at phase 8. Phase 7 deletes only what it replaces: the version-1 export-recovery object, the holder's use of `export-task`/`resume-task`, the authority's use of the version-1 inspector. The old codec and its direct tests stay green and dead until phase 8.\n5. One hard fact drives the design: `dao.jing` content stores **verify bytes against the address on put**, and a row pack's bytes do not hash to its root id. So the checkpoint has two identities: the pack address (storage, the ledger's `:yin.k/id`) and the root id (semantic, inside the baseline). `emit` must sort its rows so one root gives one pack on every host.\n6. Custody travels as the root's `custody` slot exactly as the draft's §5.5 table says (policy, occurrence, arbitration, next-op-seq, origin); lease, epoch and holder never enter a body (UCF 7.2.1 stands); op-ids already have their slots on `:put`, `:ffi-request` and `:link-request` pendings.\n7. The recovery object loses its second \"snapshot\" body (rows already carry the whole store and every parked record) and keeps only holder-local engine state (gate, issues, issued, origins) beside the pack.\n8. Crash cuts stay meaningful unchanged if the pack is stored as **one** blob; storing rows individually would add a torn-pack cut family and is not recommended.\n9. `:resume-mode` stays out: the version-2 stack lane is frozen until phase 8, and rows carry `:act`/`:tail`, which subsume it. No work.\n10. Three questions need the owner: fork spelling in the custody slot (nil as landed, or the draft's explicit `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}`); the REPL custody gap (the REPL composes the old kernel until phase 8, so its custody path is dead between 7 and 8 unless the kernel switch is pulled forward); whether the Q3 install checks (link-id = parent, image address = manifest) land in 7b.\n11. Slices: 7a custody on rows and the rows inspector with regenerated fixtures; 7b holder export, recovery v2 and the driver's lower; 7c the composition and the compose/fencing row tests; 7d the Stage E world and matrix across processes; 7e docs. 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d, with 7e and the 7d world port overlapping 7c.\n12. Estimate: 12 working days median (9 implementation, the rest 2-4 codex rounds per slice and the slow-tier runs), 17 at the worst; phase 6's three slices landed in three calendar days with the design pre-written, so calendar could be shorter.\n13. Biggest risks: the identity split surprising the ledger's variant rules (mitigated by sorting rows); the fenced-wait rehydration of install children on the semantic-register kernel, exercised only by the harness today; Dart runs only the in-process Stage E form (as today).\n14. Nothing found that forces a redesign of rows or the ledger; the de Bruijn lanes are not Stage E participants (the 61/350 matrix covers only the cross-engine laws).\n15. Verdict at the end: **NOT READY** until the owner answers the three questions in §3.10; everything else is decided below and can start on 7a the moment the fork-spelling answer lands.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Inventory: every Stage D/E scenario and test, its transport today, what it needs on rows\n\nVocabulary (UCF 7.5.3): a *cursor* is the immutable `dao.stream` value; a *cursor cell* is the carrier's box (`:yin.k/cc-<n>` in rows, `:yin.k/c-<n>` in versions 0-2); a *cursor-ref* is the sealed reference a program holds; a *cell* is only the heap box.\n\nTransport codes: **v0** = `handoff/export-task` with a nil header, stream CBOR, fork; **v1** = the same with a header, jing CBOR, exclusive; **v2** = `{:version 2}`, the four-profile body (frozen); **rec1** = the export-recovery object version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`, embeds a v0/v1/v2 body plus a full-census snapshot); **ckpt** = `checkpoint/inspect` over v1/v2 bytes; **ledger** = `yin.vm.ucf.ledger` records (no body); **rows** = `rows/lift-task`/`lower-task`, version 3.\n\n### 1.1 Stage E (the acceptance matrix, `stage_e_*`)\n\nThe world (`stage_e_world.cljc`) builds both machines with `lift-support/new-machine`, which is `yin.vm.semantic/create-vm` (old kernel). Every life drives `compose` only; the export is v1 (`:export-version` nil), the recovery object rec1, the offer carries v1 bytes, the authority inspects with ckpt. Dart runs the in-process form only; JVM and Node also run the separate-process form over the four ordered pairs.\n\n| Scenario (ids) | Count | Parking shape (continuation §5.2 row) | Transport today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `[:fork]` | 1 | row 1 (blocked read, `:parked` world) | v0 lift, no bracket | rows lift with no header → root custody nil (or fork spelling, §3.1); the driver's `fork-step` answers pack bytes and address |\n| `[:row-7 cut]` × 5 cuts | 5 | row 1 at the source; successor is the same shape | v1 export, rec1, ckpt on offer and report | rows export under header; recovery v2; rows inspector on offer and report; successor pack address as `:yin.k/result` |\n| `[:row-7 :failure-lower]`, `[:row-7 :lost-authority]` | 2 | row 1 | v1, ckpt | as above; failure-lower is `attach!` returning not-found inside `lower-task` step 11 |\n| `[:row-4 cut]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 then row 2 (`:rows` world: read, write to the enrolled target, write to the side log, halt) | v1 with an op-id on the `:put`, ckpt baseline, admission | rows lift with the enrolled set (new `:unprotected-pending`/unenrolled checks); op-id slot already exists; baseline from the rows inspector |\n| `[:row-3 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2 | v1, admission | as row 4 |\n| `[:row-5 variant]` × 2 | 2 | rows 1, 2; evicted kept cursor (gap) | v1; input records | unchanged protocol; the kept cursor position is a cursor-cell body `[stream position]` in rows |\n| `[:row-2 cut]` × 14 cuts | 14 | row 1 | v1 export phases: mint, serve, store recovery (`store-put-*`), store body (`body-put-*`), fence, offer | the same phases over the pack; see §3.4 for why the cut set stays |\n| `[:row-2-carrier outcome]` × 4 | 4 | row 1 | v1 offer appends, abort | unchanged (`export/abort` is machine-level) |\n| `[:row-1]` | 1 | row 1; a second encoding minted by rewriting `:yin.k/id-counter` in the CBOR body | v1 variant bytes | the variant must be re-emitted: decode pack → change the root's id-counter slot → `emit` → new root id and new pack; equal baseline (§3.3) |\n| `[:row-6 ...]` × 3 | 3 | row 1 | v1; partition and lost reply | unchanged |\n| `[:clause-8 variant]` × 2, `[:clause-8 :closed]` | 3 | rows 1, 2; cross-target | v1, admission, inherited ids read the accepted checkpoint through ckpt | `accepted-baseline` must inspect the stored pack with the rows inspector |\n| `[:clause-3 :regrant]`, `[:clause-3 :carried-put]` | 2 | row 2 with an id assigned before export | v1 carries the id | rows carry `:op-id` on the `:put` pending slot; the successor's counter from the custody slot |\n| `[:journal cut]` × 7, `{:fail ...}` × 2 | 9 | row 1 | v1; inbox retention, journal brackets | unchanged except the fence record's two addresses are recovery v2 and the pack |\n\nTotal: 51 scenarios (`all-scenarios`), 12 `deftest`s in `stage_e_rows_test` (8 slow) and 12 in `stage_e_process_test` (all slow, JVM only, four host pairs, Node peer compiled by shadow with the peer as main).\n\n**What the world itself needs:** `parked-reader-over` and `reading-writer` built with `yin.vm.semantic-register/create-vm` and `sr/load-vector` over `linearize/project` (as the harness's `machine`/`load-program` do), a `:source-index` supplied to the lift (the composition's `{A #{tree}}`, continuation §6.1), `holder-seams` unchanged, and `offer-variant!` re-emitting a pack instead of patching CBOR.\n\n### 1.2 Stage D (the driver, the holder halves, the compose rows)\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Transport today | What it tests | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `holder/driver_test.cljc` (118) | v0/v1/v2 through `handoff/resume-task`, `export/*`, rec1, ckpt | the whole driver: brackets, inbox, grant, lower, run, exit, reopen, abort, enrollment | machines from a semantic-register support namespace; the three driver call sites (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`/`reopen-holder`/`reopen-exit`) over rows; `a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test` deleted at 7 (its subject is the frozen v2 lane; nothing else exercises `:export-version 2` through the driver) |\n| `holder/export_test.cljc` (26) | v0/v1/v2 enter/prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate/abort | the exporting state and recovery | `enter`, `abort`, hold refusals: unchanged (machine-level); prepare/encode/freeze/rehydrate: rewritten over rows and recovery v2; the \"snapshot extends body\" and \"transitive module store fixed point\" tests disappear (rows carry everything once) |\n| `holder/writer_test.cljc` (29) | machines with a hand-set `:yin.k/custody` map; no transport | assign, envelope, retention, discharge, closes | port the machine builders to the semantic-register kernel; logic unchanged (`writer.cljc` reads engine fields only) |\n| `holder/reader_test.cljc` (24) | same | held observations, replay, frontier | same |\n| `holder/evidence_test.cljc` (10) | ledger | binding evidence | none |\n| `fencing_rows_test.cljc` (4) | v1 lift + writer + reader + authority, no compose | rows 2, 4, 5 fencing halves | rows lift under header; `lower-task` with the grant opts |\n| `compose_test.cljc` (13) | v0/v1 through compose | open!, fork label, substrate checks, reopen, the plain composition | the `:receiver` and `:machine` seams become semantic-register machines; `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` reads the REPL's wiring (see §3.10 Q2) |\n| `compose_rows_test.cljc` (41, 1 slow) | v1 through compose; rec1; ckpt | rows 1-8 same host, completion recovery, journal cuts around freeze/storage/fencing, the content-store cuts | as compose; the content-store cut tests must see two puts (recovery v2, pack) as today |\n| `lift_v1_test.cljc` (29) | v1 `export-task` | header rules, enrolled refusals, canonical bytes, cursor cells | **kept until phase 8** (tests the old codec directly); its custody rules are re-pinned on rows in a new `rows_custody_test` (7a) |\n| `handoff_v2_custody_test.cljc` (5), `handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` (6), the other `handoff_v2_*` (8 files), `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, `scalar_round_trip_test` | v0/v1/v2 codec | the old codec and layouts | kept until phase 8, except `a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced` (tests rec1 freeze, deleted at 7) and `a-poisoned-receiver-behaves-as-a-clean-one` (goes through `resume-task` only, keep) |\n\n### 1.3 Stage C (the authority), where a body enters\n\n| Test file (deftests) | Body use today | Needs on rows |\n|---|---|---|\n| `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, `checkpoint_test.cljc` (14), `test/resources/.../checkpoint-v1.txt` | five bases lifted by the old semantic kernel through v1, 48 mutations, 3 byte anchors | **kept as the old inspector's test** until phase 8; a new `rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` pins packs lifted by the semantic-register kernel (same five bases, the mutations re-expressed as row edits with rehash, §5.2's \"raw row edit\" rule) |\n| `ledger_fixtures.cljc`, `ledger_fixture_test.cljc` (6), `ledger-v1.txt` | `park`/`halted` bodies derived from the checkpoint fixtures and offered/reported through `grant/offer!` and `completion/report!` | regenerate over packs (the projection digest and every frame change); file renamed `ledger-v2.txt` |\n| `crash_cut_test.cljc` (2) | `lf/world`, `lf/park` bodies | follows the ledger fixtures |\n| `authority/grant_test.cljc` (16), `completion_test.cljc` (21), `inherited_test.cljc` (18), `front_test.cljc` (29), `admission_test.cljc` (24), `input_test.cljc` (20), `reclaim_test.cljc` (21), `completion_fold_test.cljc` (8) | bodies through `checkpoint-fixtures`/`ledger-fixtures` where an offer, report or inherited id is admitted; otherwise ledger only | the fixture switch above; the inspector call in `grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline` becomes the rows inspector |\n| `ledger_test` (14), `custody_test` (4), `durability_test` (10), `evidence_test` | ledger only | none |\n\n### 1.4 Phase 6 assets phase 7 builds on (no change expected)\n\n`rows_test.cljc` (31), `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (27; rows 1-24 of §5.2 on three lanes, laws 1-6), `frame_test`, `layout_test`. The harness's `machine`, `load-program`, `project`/`source-index` and `canonical-under` are the templates for the new support namespace.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Old transport paths: deleted at phase 7 versus kept until phase 8\n\n**Deleted or replaced at phase 7** (their subject is replaced, so keeping them would test a dead path):\n\n- `holder.export/prepare`, `encode`, `freeze`, `rehydrate-fenced` as written over `handoff/export-task`, `inspect-recovery-body` and `rehydrate-fenced-body`: rewritten over rows. `enter` and `abort` survive unchanged.\n- The export-recovery object version 1 (`:yin.k/export-recovery`, `:yin.k/version 1`, `:yin.k/snapshot-*`): replaced by version 2 (§3.2). No reader of version 1 remains.\n- The driver's calls: `handoff/resume-task` in `lower` and `step-validating`; `checkpoint/inspect` in `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`; `export/rehydrate-fenced` in `rebuilt-cell`; `:export-version` as a seam (compose's `holder-config-keys` and `driver-config` drop it). The `fork-step` keeps its shape over the rows lift.\n- The authority's three inspector calls (`grant/offer!`, `completion/report!`, `admission/accepted-baseline`) switch to the rows inspector; the ledger's `:yin.k/baseline` shape gains `:yin.k/root` (§3.3).\n- `lift-support`'s role as the D/E machine source: superseded by a semantic-register support namespace; `lift-support` itself stays for the old codec's tests.\n- Tests deleted at 7: `driver_test/a-version-two-fork-lift-is-the-composition-choice-test`, `handoff_v2_holder_test/a-version-2-recovery-snapshot-freezes-and-rehydrates-fenced`, `export_test`'s rec1-specific rows (`complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible`, `module-store-dependencies-are-censused-to-a-fixed-point`, `version-one-recovery-retains-operation-ids-issues-and-local-enrollment`, `snapshot`-shaped assertions) are replaced by their rows equivalents in the same file.\n\n**Kept until phase 8, untouched** (the brief's rule: no new work on the old kernels and compilers):\n\n- `handoff.cljc` whole (version 0/1/2 lift, reader, `validate-body`, `rehydrate-fenced-body`, `inspect-recovery-body`; the last two become dead code with no caller).\n- `checkpoint.cljc` (the version-1/2 inspector) and `checkpoint_test`/`checkpoint_fixtures`/`checkpoint-v1.txt`.\n- `remote.cljc`'s version 0-2 pending lift/lower (`lift-frame`, `lower-frame`, `lift-pending`, `lower-pending`); rows uses only `lift-marker`/`lower-marker`/`cursor-profiles`.\n- `lift_v1_test`, `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, every `handoff_v2_*` test but the one deftest above, `scalar_round_trip_test`, `v2_support`, `lift_support`, `handoff-v2.txt`, `scalars-v1.*`.\n- `custody.cljc`, `ledger.cljc`, `authority.cljc`, `authority/*`, `holder/{driver,writer,reader,inbox,evidence}.cljc`, `compose.cljc`: not old transport; they change only at the call sites listed above.\n\n**Owner rulings honoured:** A is the only code identity (rows carry `{A vector}` and `:yin.k/trees`; the inspector names code by A only); the AST is never lost (`:source-index` is a lift input in every slice; the Stage E composition must supply it); cursor-cell sharing survives transport (the census, unchanged); BLAKE3 (row ids, pack address, intent digest, recovery address all under Jing's default); no backward compatibility (no version dispatch in the driver, fixtures regenerated, files renamed); native lanes excluded from Stage E.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Design questions phase 7 forces, with recommendation, cost, and who decides\n\n### 3.1 Exclusive and holder custody on rows: which rows carry lease, fence and holder state\n\n**Finding.** The root row's slot 16 `custody` is plain data (draft §5.5 table). UCF 7.2.1 is operative for version 3: \"no body carries an epoch; the epoch is learned only from the grant binding\". The holder state the driver needs at run time (`:yin.k/custody` on the machine: occurrence, lease, holder, epoch, arbitration, next-op-seq, `:protection`, `:input {:next :prefix}`, `:tenure`) is built by the lower from the **grant evidence**, never from the body (`accept-grant!` today). The fence (op-ids) already has slots: `:put [stream V op-id]`, `:ffi-request [... op-id]`, `:link-request [... envelope op-id]`.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).**\n- The root `custody` slot carries exactly the draft's table for `:blocked`/`:parked` exclusive roots: `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/arbitration {...} :yin.k/next-op-seq n}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on a successor; a halted exclusive root carries `{:yin.k/origin ...}` only; children nil (as now). Lease, epoch, holder, enrolled set, protection classes: **never** in a row.\n- `lift-task` gains a `:header` option (the same header `holder.export/prepare` takes today). With a header it writes the slot by kind, refuses `:op-seq-exhausted` at 2^52-1 on a blocked or parked root, `:unprotected-pending` for a retained `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` on an enrolled target without an op-id (root or child), and `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream for an op-id on an unenrolled target; a halted root with a header but no origin is refused (not downgraded). These are the version-1 rules, moved (`handoff.cljc:623-657`, `:1513-1523`).\n- `lower-task` replaces `check-custody!` (nil only) by the version-1 custody stage: fork root under `:exclusive` → `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; exclusive root of kind `:blocked`/`:parked` requires `:grant` + `:protection` opts and runs the seven grant checks of `accept-grant!` (`handoff.cljc:3343-3420`, ported verbatim, keyed off the slot instead of body keys); every marker's protection class must match enrollment in both directions; op-id presence must equal enrollment per retained write; the restored machine gets `:yin.k/custody` from the evidence and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the slot **exactly** (no max); a halted exclusive root lowers with no grant, gated `:ended`. A `:fenced` option (recovery, §3.2) skips the grant and gates `:exporting`.\n- The custody stage keeps its place: all four stages over the whole tree before the first `attach!` (continuation §7.2, codex F1/F2/F4/F7).\n\n**Fork spelling (OWNER decides, §3.10 Q1).** Phase 6 writes `custody nil` for a fork root; the draft's table spells `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` on blocked/parked fork roots and nil on halted ones. Both decide the policy from the root alone. Nil is landed and every pinned root id in `rows_test` and the harness depends on it; the explicit spelling costs about half a day of pin regeneration and makes the slot's reader one `case` on `:yin.k/policy`. I recommend **keeping nil** and amending the draft's table (fork = nil in every row), on the minimal-diff principle; the owner may prefer the draft's spelling.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (lift option, three refusals, the ported grant checks, the protection stage, tests on three hosts).\n\n### 3.2 The export-recovery object re-based on rows\n\n**Finding.** Version 1 (`holder.export/freeze`) stores two bodies: the published body and a \"snapshot\" lifted with `::recovery true` (full census), because version-0 bodies omitted transitive module stores and the recovery must rehydrate everything. It also stores `:yin.k/header`, `:yin.k/descriptors` (the served set) and `:yin.k/state` (`gate`, `origins`, `issues` per wait, `issued`, children recursively). `rehydrate-fenced` restores the snapshot with `rehydrate-fenced-body` (gate `:exporting`, waits in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked table emptied into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` mapping resource ids back to carrier cursor cells), re-prepares from the retained served table and proves the published bytes are reproduced.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Version 2:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/export-recovery true\n :yin.k/version         2\n :yin.k/pack-address    a        ; the pack's own address (§3.3)\n :yin.k/root            B        ; the root id inside it\n :yin.k/pack-bytes      base64\n :yin.k/header          h        ; nil for a fork\n :yin.k/descriptors     #{...}   ; the served set, as today\n :yin.k/state           {:gate g :origins n :issued n :issues [..] :children {m state}}}\n```\n\nOne body: the pack already carries the whole root store, every parked record and the module stores by reachability (continuation §2.5), so the \"snapshot extends body\" check and the fixed-point repair disappear. `rehydrate-fenced` = decode, verify `pack-address` and `root`, `lower-task` with `{:fenced true :header h}` (no grant, gate `:exporting`, waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, parked records moved into the record, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids` from the census), then `prepare` from the retained served table and `encode` must reproduce `pack-bytes` exactly. Engine-local state that is not canonical content (`issues`, `issued`, `origins`, `gate`) stays in `:yin.k/state` as today; `:yin.k/issue` on lowered `:put` waits is assigned sequentially in carried order (what `resume-task*` does, `handoff.cljc:3643-3650`) and overwritten from `:issues` on rehydration. The recovery address stays the BLAKE3 of the recovery bytes.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7b (export.cljc rewrite, `:fenced` lower mode, export_test re-based).\n\n### 3.3 Checkpoint and ledger embedding of a row body root\n\n**Finding.** The authority stores the body bytes under `:yin.k/id` with `(:put-bytes-fn store)`, and both `dao.jing.mem` and `dao.jing.file` **refuse a put whose bytes do not hash to the address** (`mem.cljc:38`, `file.cljc:351`). The pack `{:yin.k/handoff true :yin.k/version 3 :yin.k/root B :yin.k/rows [...]}` does not hash to `B`. Further, `emit` builds `:yin.k/rows` as `(vec (vals rows))` of a hash map, so the pack's byte order is host-dependent today; `B` is not.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; the draft already allows it, §10 answer 2: \"when a pack is addressed it gets its own address, distinct from the semantic root id\").**\n- `emit` sorts `:yin.k/rows` by row id (bytewise). One root → one pack on every host. Root ids do not change, so no pin moves.\n- Two identities, each used where it belongs: the **pack address** `a = BLAKE3(pack bytes)` is the storage key and the ledger's `:yin.k/id` (a snapshot variant is an encoding, as today); the **root id** `B` is the semantic identity and enters the baseline as `:yin.k/root`. Two packs with one root are variants of equal baseline (impossible after sorting, but harmless). The offer request, the fence record and the report's `:yin.k/result` carry `a`; `offer-decision`'s `(jing/segment-address? (:yin.k/id fact))` holds for `a`.\n- The **rows inspector** (`yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint/inspect address bytes`): address matches bytes (`jing/segment-bytes-match?`), bytes decode through `rows/decode` (every row hash, canonical numbering, frames), the root's custody slot by kind (the version-1 header rules of `checkpoint.cljc:183-232` on the slot's map; children nil), every op-id on `:put`/`:ffi-request`/`:link-request` pendings in root and children against the root's occurrence, origin and counter (`check-op-id!` verbatim), duplicate ids refused, and the baseline `{:yin.k/kind :yin.k/root :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/origin :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq :yin.k/ops {op-id digest}}` where the intent digest is `BLAKE3(cbor [:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot])` with the payload **as the wire slot holds it** (`[:lit x]` or `[:row id]`, both canonical). A fork pack answers `:yin.k/fork true` as `inspect-v2` does. Every refusal is data; nothing throws (as today).\n- `admission/accepted-baseline` and `completion/successor-defect` read `:yin.k/next-op-seq` and `:yin.k/origin` from the baseline, unchanged. `ledger/offer-defect` is unchanged (`:yin.k/kind` ∈ `#{:blocked :parked}`, the baseline compared whole).\n- Halted exit: the report's `:yin.k/result` is the halted pack's address; the terminal edge names it.\n\n**Cost.** 1.5 days in 7a (inspector, sorted emit, fixtures regenerated, the three authority call sites, `ledger-v2.txt`).\n\n### 3.4 Crash cuts: which cuts are meaningful when the body is a Merkle row set\n\n**Finding.** The named cuts (`stage_e_world/cuts`) cut the progress journal around mint, serve, store intent/ack, two content-store puts (recovery first, body second), fence, offer; the authority's commit; the reply journal; and composition ticks. They are meaningful at the granularity of **one store put per object**.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides).** Store the pack as **one** content-store object. Then every cut keeps its meaning: `:export/store-put-*` cuts the recovery v2 put, `:export/body-put-*` the pack put, and a pack is either whole or absent; a torn pack is impossible by the store's atomic insert. Storing rows individually (one object per row, Merkle-addressed) would make partial checkpoints possible and require a new cut family (`:pack/missing-row-before-fence`) and a new refusal path (`rows/decode` `:missing-row` at the authority), with no benefit in phase 7. Row-level storage is a phase-8+ option for deduplication across checkpoints and is out of scope. One new cut is worth adding cheaply: `:export/pack-put-after-recovery-missing` is already covered by `store-put-before` (recovery absent, nothing fenced); no addition needed.\n\n**Cost.** None beyond keeping the two-put order in `export-fence-step`.\n\n### 3.5 Publication of successors\n\n**Finding.** The protocol (`exit-mint` → prepare → freeze → fence → report → release → closure; the successor offer refused `:awaiting-completion` then admitted) is body-agnostic except for three values: the successor header (built from the machine's `:yin.k/custody`), the report's result address and the stored bytes.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No protocol change. The successor's header takes `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from the machine's custody map as today; the lift writes it into the slot; the inspector's `:counter-regression` check compares slot against baseline. The report carries the successor's pack address; `reopen-exit` rebuilds from the journaled mint and the fence as today. The one wrinkle: `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` read the kind and occurrence from the stored bytes through the inspector; the rows inspector answers the same keys.\n\n**Cost.** Included in 7b.\n\n### 3.6 Authority and capability tokens versus sealed refs on rows\n\n**Finding.** Rows re-seal every stream and cursor-ref under the receiver's owner on lower (`engine/issue-ref`), authenticate refs on lift, and carry no credential (UCF 7.7.8: \"the envelope carries no credential; the holder is authenticated by the attribution the composition supplies\"). The owner ruled (2026-10-12) that ShiBi tokens are a configurable policy at the `:admit` seam, and (2026-09-27) that ShiBi is a tuple space emerging from index and query interpreters, with `dao.stream.remote` offering only a capability-agnostic seam.\n\n**Recommendation (Architect decides; owner to confirm scope).** Phase 7 carries **no** capability token in any row and adds no row kind for one. Authority is ledger facts, attribution is the composition's resolver, tenure is lease evidence; sealed refs are the receiver's and are minted on lower. A token-bearing admission is ShiBi work at the admission seam, after phase 8.\n\n**Cost.** None.\n\n### 3.7 `:resume-mode` under version 2\n\n**Finding.** The owner's ruling (orchestrator log, 2026-10-11) is that the version-2 stack lane stays frozen and does not carry `:resume-mode`. Rows carry `:act` and `:tail` rows; a `:tail` is the register kernel's `:return-result` delivery and an `:act` the `:write-result` one (continuation §4.2, §4.3), so the information `:resume-mode` encodes is structural in version 3.\n\n**Recommendation (decided).** No work. Phase 7 does not touch version 2; version 3 needs no such key.\n\n### 3.8 Two gaps the brief did not list but phase 7 meets\n\n- **Continuation §11 Q4 (op-id rules on lower).** Closed by the rows inspector running inside `lower-task`'s custody stage for exclusive roots (below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin, never the body's own occurrence). Fork roots refuse any op-id (`:fork-op-id`), as `inspect-v2` does. Decided, in 7a.\n- **Continuation §11 Q3 (install verification).** `check-install!` verifies status, manifest name, exports and that the image loads, not link-id = parent nor image address = the manifest's. Adding both is about a quarter day in 7b. **OWNER decides** (§3.10 Q3); recommend yes, because the recovery object now trusts the pack for install children.\n- **Continuation §11 Q1/Q10 (the `:running` wire phase).** Unchanged by phase 7; Stage E's installer scenarios use phase `:parked` only. Leave open.\n\n### 3.9 Which kernels take part\n\nStage E runs the **semantic-register kernel only** (the reference lane). The de Bruijn kernels are covered by the cross-engine laws of the harness (61/350, accepted) and are not Stage E holders or sources; the holder driver never lowers through a layout. This follows the owner's matrix ruling and avoids three times the process matrix.\n\n### 3.10 The owner's questions (blocking)\n\n- **Q1, fork spelling in the custody slot.** nil (landed; recommended) or the draft's `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` (half a day of pin regeneration; the draft is amended either way).\n- **Q2, the REPL custody gap.** `yin.repl` composes the **old** `yin.vm.semantic` kernel (`repl.cljc:144`) and wires `compose` (`repl/main.cljc:979-1011`). After phase 7 the driver reads only packs, so the REPL's custody path cannot lift its machine until phase 8 renames the semantic-register kernel into `yin.vm.semantic`. Options: (a) accept the gap until 8 (recommended: `the-plain-composition-loads-and-drives-with-no-yin-repl-test` is re-based on a semantic-register receiver, and the REPL's `:custody` branch is marked inert until 8); (b) pull the REPL's kernel switch into phase 7 (an extra slice, two to three days, touching `yin.repl` and its tests, which 4b/5 did not plan).\n- **Q3, the install checks of continuation §11 Q3** in 7b (recommended yes, a quarter day).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Slicing\n\nEach slice: files, dependencies, acceptance tests, hosts, days (opus implementation + review rounds at the observed 2-4 codex rounds per slice, about half a day per round including fixes).\n\n### 7a: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures\n\n**Create.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` (the inspector of §3.3); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` (semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support`: `new-machine` over `sr/create-vm` with `tu/make-stream`, deterministic one-slot streams, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-installer`, `parked-linker`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `with-op-id`, `serve-table`, `header`, `source-index` capture through `linearize/project`); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` + `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (the five bases lifted by `rows/lift-task` under a header, the mutations as raw row edits with rehash where they touch a row and as root-slot edits otherwise, three anchors with full pack bytes); `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc`; `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` (the lift and lower custody rules of §3.1: header by kind, the three lift refusals, the seven grant checks with zero attachments, protection in both directions, exact counter restore, halted exclusive lowers ended, fork under `:exclusive` refused).\n\n**Change.** `rows.cljc` (`emit` sorts rows by id; `lift-task` `:header`; `lower-task` custody stage with `:grant`/`:protection`/`:exclusive`/`:fenced` opts; `check-custody!` replaced); `authority/grant.cljc`, `authority/completion.cljc`, `authority/admission.cljc` (inspector call); `ledger.cljc` (none expected; `:yin.k/root` in the baseline is opaque to the fold); `ledger_fixtures.cljc` (`park`/`halted` from rows packs; `path` → `ledger-v2.txt`), `ledger_fixture_test`, `crash_cut_test`, `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test` (fixture bodies only).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; owner's Q1.\n\n**Acceptance.** `rows_checkpoint_test` (every base accepted with its baseline; every mutation refused at its path; the file equals `render` on three hosts), `rows_custody_test`, `ledger_fixture_test` (every host rebuilds every frame byte for byte; the projection digest pinned), `crash_cut_test`, the eight authority tests green, `rows_test` and the safepoint harness unchanged (no root id moves), `store_write_audit_test` allowlist updated if the row table changes.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 2.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **4**.\n\n### 7b: the holder's export record, recovery v2, the driver's lower\n\n**Change.** `holder/export.cljc` (`prepare`/`encode` over `rows/lift-task` with `:header` and a `:serve-keyed` adaptor; `freeze` → recovery v2; `rehydrate-fenced` over `lower-task {:fenced true}`); `holder/driver.cljc` (`lower`, `step-validating`, `rebuilt-cell`, `reopen-holder`, `reopen-exit`, `source-mint`/`exit-mint` unchanged in shape; `:export-version` removed); `compose.cljc` (`holder-config-keys`/`driver-config` drop `:export-version`); `holder/export_test`, `holder/driver_test`, `holder/writer_test`, `holder/reader_test` (machines from `rows_support`; rec1 rows replaced; the v2 fork-lift deftest deleted); `handoff_v2_holder_test` (one deftest deleted).\n\n**Depends on.** 7a; owner's Q3 for the install checks (a quarter day inside this slice).\n\n**Acceptance.** `export_test` (recovery v2 freezes and rehydrates fenced with zero program IO, the published pack reproduced byte for byte, aliases and distinct cursor cells preserved, install children rehydrated without initialization or IO, a restarted abort refused), `driver_test` whole (117 deftests), `writer_test`, `reader_test`, `evidence_test`; the law \"zero attachments before the custody stage refuses\" pinned by attach counts.\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart.\n\n**Days.** 3 implementation + 2 review (4 rounds; the driver is 2.9k lines and its tests 4.3k) = **5**.\n\n### 7c: the composition and the compose and fencing rows\n\n**Change.** `fencing_rows_test` (rows lift under header, `lower-task` with grant opts), `compose_test` (semantic-register `:receiver`/`:machine`; the plain-composition test per Q2), `compose_rows_test` (41 deftests; `offer-variant`-style helpers re-emit packs; content-store cuts see recovery v2 then the pack); `yin.repl/main.cljc` only if Q2 answers (b).\n\n**Depends on.** 7b; owner's Q2.\n\n**Acceptance.** All three files green on three hosts; the slow deftest `completion-recovery-unknown-policy-transaction-is-reconciled-once-test` under `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1` on each lane.\n\n**Days.** 1.5 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) = **3**.\n\n### 7d: Stage E on the semantic-register kernel, three hosts, four process pairs\n\n**Change.** `stage_e_world.cljc` (`parked-reader-over`, `reading-writer` over `rows_support`; `holder-seams` supplies `:source-index`; `offer-variant!` re-emits; `snapshot`'s `:fences` reads pack digests), `stage_e_scenarios.cljc` (checks that name `:yin.k/id-counter` or body keys adjusted; row 1's \"two encodings\" assertion keeps its meaning), `stage_e_peer.cljc` (none expected), `stage_e_process_test.clj` (none expected; the Node peer build picks up the new namespaces), `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` (none expected).\n\n**Depends on.** 7c (the world port can begin on 7b and run in-process while 7c is in review).\n\n**Acceptance.** `stage_e_rows_test` on JVM, Node and Dart (slow tier, `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`); `stage_e_process_test` on the JVM over `[[:jvm :jvm] [:jvm :node] [:node :jvm] [:node :node]]`, every life its own process, every cut a SIGKILL, `target/stage-e-durability.edn` appended; the fast-lane gate `the-cross-host-handoff-across-processes-test` first. Row 10's canonical-byte agreement across hosts now holds for packs as well (sorted rows).\n\n**Hosts.** JVM, Node, Dart (in-process); JVM↔Node (processes).\n\n**Days.** 2 implementation + 1.5 review (3 rounds) + 0.5 for the slow matrix runs (the process matrix is about an hour per full pass on the remote hosts, per build-n-test) = **4**.\n\n### 7e: documentation\n\n**Change.** `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md` §2.5, §2.6 (custody slot by kind), §7.1-7.2 (the `:header` lift, the custody and fenced lower stages), §8.4 (phase 7 met), §10 (a 10.7 inventory), §11 (Q3, Q4 closed; Q1/Q10 left); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §5.5 (the custody table as ruled), §7 (pack address versus root id, sorted rows), §9 (the fenced lower); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` 7.7.4 (recovery v2 paragraph replacing the version-1 one), 7.11.1 clauses 1-5 and 10 (stage letters updated); `docs/orchestrator-log.md` entry.\n\n**Depends on.** The owner's answers; concurrent with 7c/7d.\n\n**Days.** 1 + 1 review round = **1.5**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risks and what could force a redesign\n\n1. **The identity split (pack address versus root id).** If the owner or a reviewer insists the ledger's `:yin.k/id` be the root id, the content store contract must change (a verifier-taking put) or the authority must store under the pack address and record both; the second is what §3.3 recommends anyway, so the redesign cost is in naming, not mechanics. Sorting rows removes the host-order variance that would otherwise make JVM and Node packs distinct variants.\n2. **Fenced rehydration on the semantic-register kernel.** `rehydrate-fenced-body` relies on the version-0/1/2 lower's `:fenced?` arm (`handoff.cljc:3688-3699`, `:3722-3725`): gate `:exporting`, waits parked in `:yin.k/recovery-waits`, `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the FFI pair resources for `:ffi-request` pendings, `id-counter` carried exactly. `lower-task` has none of this; porting it is routine but the install-child case (children exporting, their parked records moved to the child's record) is exercised only by `export_test`. Medium.\n3. **Install children on the semantic-register kernel under the holder.** The harness proves rows 7-12 (4b stage 2) for lift/lower; the holder's reader (held observations, replay through child call ids) over semantic-register install children has no test today. If `reader.cljc`'s walk assumes a field the new kernel spells differently, 7b finds it. Low-medium: both kernels share `engine.cljc` for `:installs`, `:wait-set`, `:yin.k/held`.\n4. **The REPL gap (Q2).** If the owner wants the REPL's custody demo live between 7 and 8, phase 7 grows by a slice and touches the 4b/5 \"no REPL edit\" boundary.\n5. **Stage E's pinned row-5 behaviour** (\"the exposed gap marker is the first and only commit\", pending an Architect ruling) is inherited unchanged; a ruling during phase 7 would change `row5`'s check, not the transport.\n6. **Dart.** Stage E runs in-process only on Dart (as today); the numbering budget (`default-budget`) is far above Stage E's task sizes. The known Dart traps (reader whitespace, `for` over chunked seqs, multi-key `assoc` on nil, duplicate protocol params) apply to the new support namespace; the harness already avoids them.\n7. **Review load.** Phase 6b took three codex rounds on a 1.8k-line file; 7b touches the 2.9k-line driver through eight call sites plus `export.cljc` whole. Budgeted at four rounds.\n8. **What would force a redesign of rows:** a requirement that the body's identity be a byte address (contradicts Merkle rows; §3.3 resolves it with two identities); a requirement to carry lease or epoch in the body (contradicts UCF 7.2.1, not proposed); row-level storage with partial checkpoints (deferred, §3.4). None is live.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Order of work and concurrency\n\n```\n7a (custody, inspector, fixtures)        ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7b (export record, recovery v2, driver)  ──────────┐\n                                                   ▼\n7c (compose, compose/fencing rows)       ──────┐   ├── 7d world port may start here\n                                               ▼   ▼\n7d (Stage E, in-process three hosts, then the process matrix)\n7e (docs) ─ starts after the owner's answers, lands beside 7c/7d\n```\n\n- 7a is the only slice that needs an owner answer before it starts (Q1). Q2 gates one deftest in 7c and possibly an extra slice; Q3 gates a quarter day in 7b.\n- Concurrent work: 7e with 7c/7d; the 7d world port (`stage_e_world` over `rows_support`) with 7c's review, since it depends on 7b's `export`/`driver` only. The old-codec tests are never touched, so no conflict with other worktrees; `rows.cljc` is the one file 7a and the harness share, and 7a keeps every pinned root id.\n- Landing cadence (owner's tiers): quick tier at every checkpoint; the slow tier (Stage E, the completion-recovery slow deftest, the process matrix) before each merge of 7c and 7d and once at the phase boundary; one lane set per machine, Dart in its own clone on the remote host.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Honest total\n\n| Slice | Implementation (opus) | Review rounds | Days |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7a | 2.5 | 3 | 4 |\n| 7b | 3 | 4 | 5 |\n| 7c | 1.5 | 3 | 3 |\n| 7d | 2 | 3 (+0.5 matrix runs) | 4 |\n| 7e | 1 | 1 | 1.5 |\n| **Total** | **10** | | **17.5 sequential** |\n\nWith 7e concurrent and the 7d port overlapping 7c, the critical path is 7a → 7b → 7c → 7d: about **14 days** of work; **12 days median** if reviews average the lower end (two rounds on 7a/7c), **17 at the worst** (four rounds everywhere, one redesign loop on the identity split). Phase 6's three slices went from first commit to merge in three calendar days with the design already written and reviews back-to-back; phase 7 has four code slices over twice the surface, so calendar time of one to two weeks is realistic if codex capacity holds.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**NOT READY.** Blocking questions for the owner: **Q1** fork spelling in the custody slot (nil, recommended, or the draft's explicit map); **Q2** the REPL custody gap until phase 8 (accept, recommended, or pull the REPL kernel switch into phase 7); **Q3** the install link-id and image-address checks in 7b (recommended yes). Every other design point above is decided by the Architect within the owner's rulings and is ready for an implementation brief for 7a on receipt of Q1.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
